### Scoring Rationale

HUBBLE is a well-executed model-suite paper occupying a clearly identified niche: a causally controlled testbed for LLM memorization research. Its core contributions—randomized insertions across real-world risk domains, causal identification of dilution and ordering effects, full open-source release at 1B–8B scale—are genuine and timely. It significantly exceeds LAMDA (6.7) in experimental design sophistication and causal rigor, with comparable significance and clarity. The most important weaknesses are: (1) no error bars or statistical tests on the key figures, (2) ELLie dataset confound, (3) scale gap to frontier LLMs limits direct extrapolation, and (4) no dedicated limitations/ethics section. These are real but do not undermine the core contribution.

Placing it above LAMDA (6.7) but below "Scaling with Collapse" (8.0), and reflecting the genuine strengths as a controlled community resource for a high-stakes domain, a score in the **6.8–7.2** range is appropriate.

