| DeepCritic | 4.7 | Reject |
| Math reasoning multiplicity | 4.0 | Reject |
| CoT deletion for physics | 3.0 | Reject |
| SFT without overfitting | 1.5 | Reject |

The current paper is a focused, well-executed ablation of GRPO components. It lacks the methodological innovation of RLKV, the theoretical depth of the Fano paper, and the breadth of LRT. The proposed RGRA is incremental. Missing the KL ablation is a notable gap for a paper whose headline is "simplification." The scope limited to small math models is a real constraint.

The paper is stronger than the "SFT without overfitting" (1.5) and "CoT deletion" (3.0) papers due to cleaner experimental design and multi-model results. It is slightly below the "Math reasoning multiplicity" paper (4.0) in novelty of finding (that paper identifies a previously unknown failure mode; this paper confirms removing a component is fine), but comparable in rigor. It falls between 4.0 and 4.5 — closer to RLKV and DeepCritic territory but not quite reaching them given the more incremental nature of the contribution.

